# {{serviceName}}: generated MCP server

Exposes every SOAP operation of {{serviceName}} as an MCP tool over stdio. Each tool call is
forwarded as JSON to the generated proxy (`../proxy`), which calls the SOAP endpoint. Start the
generated proxy first.

Tools:

{{#tools}}
- `{{name}}`: {{description}}
{{/tools}}

## Build

Requires Java 21 and Maven 3.9+.

```
mvn package
```

This produces `target/{{artifactId}}-1.0.0.jar`.

## Use it from Kiro

Add this to `.kiro/settings/mcp.json` (workspace) or `~/.kiro/settings/mcp.json` (user), with the
absolute path to the jar:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{mcpKey}}": {
      "command": "java",
      "args": ["-jar", "/absolute/path/to/mcp-server/target/{{artifactId}}-1.0.0.jar"],
      "env": { "PROXY_URL": "http://localhost:8080" }
    }
  }
}
```

`PROXY_URL` is where the generated proxy listens (default `http://localhost:8080`).

## Notes

- Tool inputs are checked by the generated proxy before anything reaches SOAP: an unknown field,
  a missing required field or a wrong type comes back as an error naming its JSON path.
- Tool descriptions quote the WSDL's documentation as data. Read them before letting an agent act
  on a backend you do not control.
- Decimal fields are JSON numbers and keep every digit (no floating point on the way to SOAP).
- Errors (invalid input, SOAP faults, an unreachable proxy) come back as tool results with
  `isError: true` and a readable message.
- stdout carries the MCP protocol; logs go to stderr.
